\section{Missingness is a column, never an absent row}{
A row is not dropped because its value is missing. \code{missing_state} says why a
value is what it is, and the distinction it protects is the one this package
cares most about: an \strong{isolate} has zero alters and an exposure genuinely
equal to zero (\code{structural_zero}), while a node whose alter went unobserved has
no defined exposure at all (\code{missing}). Both print as an unremarkable number or
a blank; they are not the same finding. A plot that silently dropped \code{NA} rows
would be doing listwise deletion in a costume.

\code{undetermined} is not a hedge — it means the object cannot answer the question.
Whether a network was \emph{observed} at a given wave is sometimes not recoverable
from a \code{netis_data} object, and rnetis reports that rather than guessing.
